    "content": "Vanuatu, officially the Republic of Vanuatu, is a South Pacific island nation in Melanesia, comprised of 83 volcanic islands with its capital and largest city, Port Vila, on Efate. It is known for its vibrant culture, lush rainforests, and top-rated diving spots. The country is highly vulnerable to natural disasters, including earthquakes and cyclones.Key Facts & GeographyLocation: Approx. 1,750 km east of northern Australia, west of Fiji.Population: Approximately 326,740 people.Languages: Bislama (official), English (official), French (official).Currency: Vanuatu Vatu (VUV).Geography: Primarily mountainous with a tropical rainforest climate, including several active volcanoes.Capital and Main IslandsPort Vila: The capital and main economic hub located on Efate island.Espiritu Santo: The largest island, known for diving and beaches.Tanna: Known for the active Mount Yasur volcano.Culture and EconomyCulture: Primarily Ni-Vanuatu (98.5%) with a high percentage of Christianity, featuring strong traditional customs.Economy: Relies on agriculture, tourism, and some offshore financial services.Infrastructure: Many residents live in rural areas practising subsistence agriculture.Tourism and ActivitiesAttractions: World-renowned scuba diving (including the SS President Coolidge wreck), swimming in blue holes in Santo, and visiting Mount Yasur on Tanna.Highlights: Underwater post office, Champagne Beach, and vibrant local markets.HistoryFormerly known as the New Hebrides, it was jointly managed by Britain and France before gaining independence in 1980.Travel InfoTravel Advisory: Generally, visitors should exercise normal precautions, with international flights available from Australia, New Zealand, and Fiji. Vanuatu, officially the Republic of Vanuatu, is a South Pacific island nation in Melanesia, comprised of 83 volcanic islands with its capital and largest city, Port Vila, on Efate. It is known for its vibrant culture, lush rainforests, and top-rated diving spots. The country is highly vulnerable to natural disasters, including earthquakes and cyclones. Key Facts & Geography Location: Approx. 1,750 km east of northern Australia, west of Fiji. Population: Approximately 326,740 people. Languages: Bislama (official), English (official), French (official). Currency: Vanuatu Vatu (VUV). Geography: Primarily mountainous with a tropical rainforest climate, including several active volcanoes. Capital and Main Islands Port Vila: The capital and main economic hub located on Efate island. Espiritu Santo: The largest island, known for diving and beaches. Tanna: Known for the active Mount Yasur volcano. Culture and Economy Culture: Primarily Ni-Vanuatu (98.5%) with a high percentage of Christianity, featuring strong traditional customs. Economy: Relies on agriculture, tourism, and some offshore financial services. Infrastructure: Many residents live in rural areas practising subsistence agriculture. Tourism and Activities Attractions: World-renowned scuba diving (including the SS President Coolidge wreck), swimming in blue holes in Santo, and visiting Mount Yasur on Tanna. Highlights: Underwater post office, Champagne Beach, and vibrant local markets. History Formerly known as the New Hebrides, it was jointly managed by Britain and France before gaining independence in 1980. Travel Info Travel Advisory: Generally, visitors should exercise normal precautions, with international flights available from Australia, New Zealand, and Fiji.",
